      "completion_tokens": 1144,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Structure\nWater is a molecule, meaning it is a group of atoms bonded together. The oxygen atom is larger and more \"electronegative\" (greedy for electrons) than the hydrogen atoms. This causes the oxygen to pull the shared electrons toward itself, creating a slight negative charge on one side of the molecule and a slight positive charge on the other. This is called **polarity**.\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties\nBecause of its polarity, water has several unique physical traits that are rare in nature:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. This is why it is the main transport medium in the human body (carrying nutrients and waste) and in the environment (dissolving minerals in soil).\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick to one another very strongly. This creates a \"skin\" on the surface of water, which allows small insects to walk across ponds without sinking.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before getting hot, and release a lot of heat before getting cold. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth (oceans) and our bodies.\n\n### 3. States of Matter\nWater is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, the molecules move less and form a rigid crystal lattice. Interestingly, ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking and washing.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air as vapor.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is essential for life as we know it. It makes up about 60% of the human body and is required for:\n*   **Cellular function:** Chemical reactions inside cells happen in water.\n*   **Temperature regulation:** Sweating and panting cool us down.\n*   **Lubrication:** It keeps our joints and eyes moist."
